# 15. Notification

## Purpose

Communicates a business event to a user or business without modifying the underlying business data.

## Owner

System-generated on behalf of the event source.

Recipient user/business owns read-state.

## Participants

- Triggering Business Event
- Recipient User(s)
- Possibly recipient Business inbox

## Required Fields

- Notification Type
- Recipient
- Title / Summary
- Trigger Event Reference
- Priority
- Created At
- Read Status

## Optional Fields

- Body Text
- Destination Reference (Order, Invoice, Payment, etc.)
- Expiry At
- Action Required Flag
- Delivery Channels (in-app, etc.)

## Relationships

- References a Business Event / Entity
- Belongs to Recipient User or Business
- Never owns financial truth

## Valid Statuses

- Unread
- Read
- Archived
- Expired
- Dismissed

## Allowed Actions

- Generate (system)
- Mark read
- Archive
- Dismiss
- Open destination
- View history

## Forbidden Actions

- Using notification as approval mechanism
- Deleting a notification in a way that deletes business records
- Editing historical trigger meaning

## Lifecycle

```
Generated (Unread)
      ↓
Read / Dismissed / Archived
      ↓
Expired (optional)
```

## Business Rules

- Notifications never create business events.
- Notification failure must not roll back the business event by default.
- Clicking may navigate to source entity if permitted.
- Priority affects presentation, not business validity.

## Audit Fields

- Created At
- Read At
- Archived At
- Event Reference

## Derived Data

- Unread Count
- Action Required Count

## Dependencies

- Source business event
- Valid recipient

## Deletion Rules

- Notifications may expire or be archived.
- Business source records remain untouched.
- Retention policy may remove old notifications without affecting transactions.

## Visibility Rules

- Only recipient (and admin for support) can view the notification instance.
- Content must not leak private cross-business data beyond recipient entitlements.

